7t\\end{aligned}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041770/state.json" "file_sha256" "2aef1fad50ee8c569cbfa51288a76516d58d4b185ea82ae78cbdc5a034a77741" "basis" 1538 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041770/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-236991" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Fill in the missing parts in the parametric equations below that describe all the points on the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(2,1,0)$ and $Q(3,2,-1).$\n\n| $x=$ | $2+t$ | $t\\in [$ {{field-1}} $,1]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y=$ | {{field-2}} |  |\n| $z=$ | $-t$ |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041770/state.json" "file_sha256" "2aef1fad50ee8c569cbfa51288a76516d58d4b185ea82ae78cbdc5a034a77741" "basis" 1538 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041770/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-236991" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the line passing through the given points. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ t \\\\ -t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2+t \\\\ 1+t \\\\ -t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nTo get the equations for the line segment, we take only $t\\in [0,1].$ Notice that substitution of $t=0$ in the above equation gives the coordinates of $P$ and substitution of $t=1$ gives the coordinates of $Q.$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ are the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2+t \\\\ y=1+t \\\\ z=-t\\end{aligned}\\,\\,t\\in [0,1]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041770/state.json" "file_sha256" "2aef1fad50ee8c569cbfa51288a76516d58d4b185ea82ae78cbdc5a034a77741" "basis" 1538 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041770/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-236991" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041770/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "04193a0fd5e3f8f0e92397cd0d61924f8c1f3a77c107660b594317d63d54a1a8" "basis" 1538 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041770/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-236991" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041770/state.json" "file_sha256" "2aef1fad50ee8c569cbfa51288a76516d58d4b185ea82ae78cbdc5a034a77741" "basis" 1538 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041770/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-236991" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041770/state.json" "file_sha256" "2aef1fad50ee8c569cbfa51288a76516d58d4b185ea82ae78cbdc5a034a77741" "basis" 1538 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041770/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-236982" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Fill in the missing parts in the parametric equations below that describe all the points on the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(-4,-2,4)$ and $Q(0,8,3).$\n\n| $x=$ | {{field-1}} | $t\\in [$ {{field-2}} $,1]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y=$ | $-2+10t$ |  |\n| $z=$ | {{field-3}} |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041770/state.json" "file_sha256" "2aef1fad50ee8c569cbfa51288a76516d58d4b185ea82ae78cbdc5a034a77741" "basis" 1538 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041770/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-236982" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the line passing through the given points. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4t \\\\ 10t \\\\ -t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-4+4t \\\\ -2+10t \\\\ 4-t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nTo get the equations for the line segment, we take only $t\\in [0,1].$ Notice that substitution of $t=0$ in the above equation gives the coordinates of $P$ and substitution of $t=1$ gives the coordinates of $Q.$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ are the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-4+4t \\\\ y=-2+10t \\\\ z=4-t\\end{aligned}\\,\\,t\\in [0,1]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041770/state.json" "file_sha256" "2aef1fad50ee8c569cbfa51288a76516d58d4b185ea82ae78cbdc5a034a77741" "basis" 1538 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041770/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-236982" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041770/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "04193a0fd5e3f8f0e92397cd0d61924f8c1f3a77c107660b594317d63d54a1a8" "basis" 1538 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041770/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-236982" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041770/state.json" "file_sha256" "2aef1fad50ee8c569cbfa51288a76516d58d4b185ea82ae78cbdc5a034a77741" "basis" 1538 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041770/edb-import/verification.json