47721dec71d3ce6c7fcf"} {"question" "q-236992" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the line passing through the given points. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2t \\\\ 4t \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4+2t \\\\ 1+4t \\\\ -1+2t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nTo get the equations for the line segment, we take only $t\\in [0,1].$ Notice that substitution of $t=0$ in the above equation gives the coordinates of $P$ and substitution of $t=1$ gives the coordinates of $Q.$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ are the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=4+2t \\\\ y=1+4t \\\\ z=-1+2t\\end{aligned}\\,\\,t\\in [0,1]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083350/state.json" "file_sha256" "8699f9215a70017c858b7baf13de3401e0afd6f3ccd047721dec71d3ce6c7fcf"} {"question" "q-236992" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083350/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4d7b16ddaac7c6a0f615f35b49c381df7fb4526c819aeb36121b03237aeaca04"} {