he line passing through the given points. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ t \\\\ -t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2+t \\\\ 1+t \\\\ -t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nTo get the equations for the line segment, we take only $t\\in [0,1].$ Notice that substitution of $t=0$ in the above equation gives the coordinates of $P$ and substitution of $t=1$ gives the coordinates of $Q.$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ are the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2+t \\\\ y=1+t \\\\ z=-t\\end{aligned}\\,\\,t\\in [0,1]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083350/state.json" "file_sha256" "8699f9215a70017c858b7baf13de3401e0afd6f3ccd047721dec71d3ce6c7fcf"} {"question" "q-236991" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083350/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4d7b16ddaac7c6a0f615f35b49c381df7fb4526c819aeb36121b03237aeaca04"} {"question" "q-236991" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083350/state.json" "file_sha256" "8699f9215a70017c858b7baf13de3401e0afd6f3ccd047721dec71d3ce6c7fcf"} {"question" "q-236991" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083350/state.json" "file_sha256" "8699f9215a70017c858b7baf13de3401e0afd6f3ccd047721dec71d3ce6c7fcf"} {"question" "q-236991" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083350/state.json" "file_sha256" "8699f9215a70017c858b7baf13de3401e0afd6f3ccd047721dec71d3ce6c7fcf"} {"question" "q-236991" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1+t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083350/state.json" "file_sha256" "8699f9215a70017c858b7baf13de3401e0afd6f3ccd047721dec71d3ce6c7fcf"} {"question" "q-236982" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Fill in the missing parts in the parametric equations below that describe all the points on the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(-4,-2,4)$ and $Q(0,8,3).$\n\n| $x=$ | {{field-1}} | $t\\in [$ {{field-2}} $,1]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y=$ | $-2+10t$ |  |\n| $z=$ | {{field-3}} |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083350/state.json" "file_sha256" "8699f9215a70017c858b7baf13de3401e0afd6f3ccd047721dec71d3ce6c7fcf"} {"question" "q-236982" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the line passing through the given points. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4t \\\\ 10t \\\\ -t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-4+4t \\\\ -2+10t \\\\ 4-t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nTo get the equations for the line segment, we take only $t\\in [0,1].$ Notice that substitution of $t=0$ in the above equation gives the coordinates of $P$ and substitution of $t=1$ gives the coordinates of $Q.$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ are the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-4+4t \\\\ y=-2+10t \\\\ z=4-t\\end{aligned}\\,\\,t\\in [0,1]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083350/state.json" "file_sha256" "8699f9215a70017c858b7baf13de3401e0afd6f3ccd047721dec71d3ce6c7fcf"} {"question" "q-236982" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083350/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4d7b16ddaac7c6a0f615f35b49c381df7fb4526c819aeb36121b03237aeaca04"} {"question" "q-236982" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083350/state.json" "file_sha256" "8699f9215a70017c858b7baf13de3401e0afd6f3ccd047721dec71d3ce6c7fcf"} {"question" "q-236982" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4+4t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083350/state.json" "file_sha256" "8699f9215a70017c858b7baf13de3401e0afd6f3ccd047721dec71d3ce6c7fcf"} {"question" "q-236982" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083350/state.json" "file_sha256" "8699f9215a70017c858b7baf13de3401e0afd6f3ccd047721dec71d3ce6c7fcf"} {"question" "q-236982" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083350/state.json" "file_sha256" "8699f9215a70017c858b7baf13de3401e0afd6f3ccd047721dec71d3ce6c7fcf"} {"question" "q-236982" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083350/state.json" "file_sha256" "8699f9215a70017c858b7baf13de3401e0afd6f3ccd047721dec71d3ce6c7fcf"} {"question" "q-236982" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4-t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083350/state.json" "file_sha256" "8699f9215a70017c858b7baf13de3401e0afd6f3ccd047721dec71d3ce6c7fcf"} {"question" "q-236986" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Fill in the missing parts in the parametric equations below that describe all the points on the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(-5,7,1)$ and $Q(-4,-1,6).$\n\n| $x=$ | {{field-1}} | $t\\in [$ {{field-2}} $,1]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y=$ | $7-8t$ |  |\n| $z=$ | $1+5t$ |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083350/state.json" "file_sha256" "8699f9215a70017c858b7baf13de3401e0afd6f3ccd047721dec71d3ce6c7fcf"} {"question" "q-236986" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the line passing through the given points. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ -8t \\\\ 5t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-5+t \\\\ 7-8t \\\\ 1+5t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nTo get the equations for the line segment, we take only $t\\in [0,1].$ Notice that substitution of $t=0$ in the above equation gives the coordinates of $P$ and substitution of $t=1$ gives the coordinates of $Q.$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ are the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-5+t \\\\ y=7-8t \\\\ z=1+5t\\end{aligned}\\,\\,t\\in [0,1]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083350/state.json" "file_sha256" "8699f9215a70017c858b7baf13de3401e0afd6f3ccd047721dec71d3ce6c7fcf"} {"question" "q-236986" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083350/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4d7b16ddaac7c6a0f615f35b49c381df7fb4526c819aeb36121b03237aeaca04"} {"question" "q-236986" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083350/state.json" "file_sha256" "8699f9215a70017c858b7baf13de3401e0afd6f3ccd047721dec71d3ce6c7fcf"} {"question" "q-236986" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-5+t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083350/state.json" "file_sha256" "8699f9215a70017c858b7baf13de3401e0afd6f3ccd047721dec71d3ce6c7fcf"} {"question" "q-236986" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083350/state.json" "file_sha256" "8699f9215a70017c858b7baf13de3401e0afd6f3ccd047721dec71d3ce6c7fcf"} {"question" "q-236986" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083350/state.json" "file_sha256" "8699f9215a70017c858b7baf13de3401e0afd6f3ccd047721dec71d3ce6c7fcf"} {"question" "e-15602" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the straight line given by the following parametric equations: \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083350/assets/a664a3af1ba466491d209dadd32a3be026a5179ac2d0281b55f30917f9ab016b.png)\nWhat would be the vector equation of the line?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083350/example-15602.json" "file_sha256" "ca2a9b7c287714d7867fada6c9f0aab3cebe4a4c79b21408a969fb046cc4af7c"} {"question" "e-15602" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "These parametric equations are equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2+2t \\\\ 5t \\\\ 1-t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2t \\\\ 5t \\\\ -t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083350/example-15602.json" "file_sha256" "ca2a9b7c287714d7867fada6c9f0aab3cebe4a4c79b21408a969fb046cc4af7c"} {"question" "e-15603" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the straight line given by the following parametric equations:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083350/assets/f161e07945eb3fe2350a56ab5cb96fa0ae2d055eb3b4cc633abfae30839ec76f.png)\nFind a vector that is parallel to the line." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083350/example-15603.json" "file_sha256" "a900dfd8af50a13ffae96462a5722da7b79e5e5d26a73cd2adeee6b8015da8c9"} {"question" "e-15603" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5-t \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-t \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nThis implies that the line passes through the point $(5,3,1)$ and is parallel to the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083350/assets/11701a2d63851a3f241d8bd905693abaaa6eda6fe4ba0c5710c92d8189595ffd.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083350/example-15603.json" "file_sha256" "a900dfd8af50a13ffae96462a5722da7b79e5e5d26a73cd2adeee6b8015da8c9"} {"question" "e-15604" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the parametric equations of the line shown in the figure below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083350/assets/53386f04f4025fda6ab579113a8ecb53a19f4dc0a77903adc05bac3a71429717.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083350/example-15604.json" "file_sha256" "daf9d19fbc342b79ed331c2d66ee0aa3bf274fe28b029f1efccbddfec9047520"} {"question" "e-15604" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We know that the line passes through the points $P(3,3,-5)$ and $Q(1,0,-1)$. Now, we need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2t \\\\ -3t \\\\ 4t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3-2t \\\\ 3-3t \\\\ -5+4t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$ And finally, the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3-2t \\\\ y=3-3t \\\\ z=-5+4t\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083350/example-15604.json" "file_sha256" "daf9d19fbc342b79ed331c2d66ee0aa3bf274fe28b029f1efccbddfec9047520"} {"question" "e-15605" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the parametric equations that describe all the points on the line segment $\\overline{PQ},$ where $P(1,-5,3)$ and $Q(0, 4, -4).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083350/example-15605.json" "file_sha256" "df4b5513f4807092bcd20b64eb39cfbbdd6ba1a104510bfc7b21e3f6c55368bd"} {"question" "e-15605" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the line passing through the given points. We can use the vector $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-t \\\\ 9t \\\\ -7t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1-t \\\\ -5+9t \\\\ 3-7t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$ \n\nTo get the equations for the line segment, we take only $t \\in [0,1].$ Notice that substitution of $t=0$ in the above equation gives the coordinates of $P$ and substitution of $t=1$ gives the coordinates of $Q.$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the line segment $\\overline{PQ}$ are the following:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083350/assets/c3747cbb9a88a419d6808959c490089c79041cc8773651fac1953f4846839c14.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083350/example-15605.json" "file_sha256" "df4b5513f4807092bcd20b64eb39cfbbdd6ba1a104510bfc7b21e3f6c55368bd"} {"question" "q-36023" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "r=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083350/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "8261d7885465848a615f861bf5425028356948db9cdc849c43977224bb5ed581" "reviewer_session" "01a108cd-e7f4-73f3-b8fc-c82135bc6b7d" "rationale" "The original prompt gives x=5, y=4t, z=7. Thus the position vector is (5,0,7)+t(0,4,0), for every real t, exactly original option e. The authentic worked solution and screenshots explicitly derive this equation. The saved Incorrect grade concerns submitted option c, r=t(5,0,7), and does not contradict this derived judgment. The historical bmatrix key has identical components, parameter and domain; its difference is presentation only." "bindings" {"problem" "6d199c5a75582a45df46f19656a0e2df0affbad412e2de09380e2e92199d44c4" "worked_solution" "aa0010b7ec2fb06dec7ade1f4427bb51bf5fb2e